# Article

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

The HTML `article` element marks a self-contained piece of content that would still make sense if it were moved to another page, such as a help note, a news item or a message. It is not a generic box for a card.

This page owns the `article` element. Home Assistant has no article component. Its nearest native part is the card (`ha-card`), which is a container, not an article.

This page covers:

- Article
  - `<article>`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

An article holds ordinary content. It should normally start with a heading (`h1` to `h6`). It may also hold a date, written in a `time` element, and short paragraphs. An article may sit inside another article, for example a comment inside a message.

It has the role `article`. It is not a landmark, and how screen readers announce it varies. It has no visible style by default and no theme variables.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-article"><code>&lt;article&gt;</code></h3>

It accepts only the global attributes. To name it, point `aria-labelledby` at its heading:

```html
<article aria-labelledby="note-title">
  <h2 id="note-title">Heating schedule changed</h2>
  <p><time datetime="2026-11-24">24 November 2026</time></p>
  <p>The evening schedule now starts at 5pm.</p>
</article>
```

**In a Home Assistant Markdown card.** The card allows `article`, but drops every attribute. The `id`, `class`, `style` and `aria-labelledby` are removed, so you cannot name an article there. Start it with a heading instead, or write the content without the element.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- It tells software the content is self-contained.
- It needs no integration and no styling.
- It does not add a landmark, so it does not help people jump around the page.
- Without a heading it has no name and little value.
- In a Markdown card it cannot be labelled.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Use an article for one idea that stands alone. Put the heading first, then a date if it matters, then short paragraphs. Do not use it for a part of a bigger whole (use `section`), for supporting remarks (use [aside](aside.html)) or as a decorative wrapper with no heading. For card structure, see [Cards](cards.html).

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: the heading and content belong together.
- 2.4.6 Headings and Labels: the heading says what the article is about.
- 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value: where `aria-labelledby` is allowed, it names the article.
- 2.4.10 Section Headings (AAA): sections have headings.

One idea, a heading first and short paragraphs keep the effort low.

